class Solution {
    public int romanToInt(String s) {
        HashMap<Character,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();
        map.put('I',1);
        map.put('V',5);
        map.put('X',10);
        map.put('L',50);
        map.put('C',100);
        map.put('D',500);
        map.put('M',1000);
        int sum=0;
        for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){
            int current=map.get(s.charAt(i));
            if(i<s.length()-1 && current<map.get(s.charAt(i+1))){
                sum-=current;
            }else{
                sum+=current;
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }
}

Explantion:
We store all Roman symbols and their integer values in a HashMap.
Then we traverse the string from left to right and compare the current symbol with the next symbol.
If the current value is smaller than the next value, we subtract it because it forms cases like IV = 4 or IX = 9.
Otherwise, we add the value normally.
Finally, the accumulated sum gives the integer equivalent of the Roman numeral.
